---
// THE PRINT ARTIFACT. Not a page you read — a page you print, or open on a
// second screen while you work. Deliberately almost wordless: everything that
// explains the method lives on the procedure page, and duplicating it here
// would give two places for the instructions to disagree.
//
// THE TABLE IS NOT SECRET. It is a deterministic mapping of the published
// BIP-39 wordlist that anyone can regenerate, so there is no risk in printing
// it, photographing it, or keeping it on a phone. Only the reader's ROLLS and
// the WORDS they produce are secret. Do not add a warning here — a false
// warning teaches readers to discount the real one on the procedure page.
//
// Every row is derived at build time and the generator asserts the table is
// bijective onto the wordlist, so a broken table fails the build.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { diceVerified } from '../data/dice.js';
import { blocks, rows, tableRowCount, DICE_PER_WORD, PREFIX_THROWS, seedCosts } from '../data/dice-table.js';
import { pdfHref } from '../data/pdf-version.js';

// ONE WORKSHEET PER SEED LENGTH, built from seedCosts — never typed. A
// worksheet with the wrong number of rows is a seed with the wrong number of
// words, and `chosen` already knows the last one is not rolled.
const WORKSHEETS = seedCosts.map((c, i, all) => ({
  words: c.words,
  chosen: c.chosen,
  other: all[(i + 1) % all.length].words,
}));

// THE WORKED EXAMPLE, looked up in the real table so it cannot disagree with
// the pages it teaches. Chosen for legibility rather than at random: no
// repeated digits in the block prefix, so the three-then-two split is visible
// at a glance, and a short common word.
const EG = rows.find((r) => r.dice === '23142' && r.coin === 'H') || rows[0];

// Dice faces for the column headers. ONLY 1–4 EVER: 5s and 6s are rerolled and
// appear nowhere in this table, so printing a ⚄ or ⚅ on our own artifact would
// contradict the method sheet two pages earlier. The coin is spelled, because
// no unicode coin reads as one at 7pt.
const PIPS = { block: '⚂⚀⚃', line: '⚁⚃', coin: 'H or T' };

// Die faces for the example line. The example used to print DIGITS in bordered
// boxes — the same shape as a row the reader fills in — so at a glance it read
// as somebody's actual first word sitting on their blank sheet. Dice and a coin
// are objects; nobody mistakes a drawn die for their own handwriting.
const FACE = { '1': '⚀', '2': '⚁', '3': '⚂', '4': '⚃' };
const egFaces = (str) => str.split('').map((d) => FACE[d]);

---
<Base
  title="BIP-39 word table (printable) · BitcoinKeys.guide"
  description="A printable table turning five dice throws and a coin flip into a BIP-39 word. Derived from the standard wordlist. Print it before you begin."
  verified={diceVerified}
>
  <section class="prose dwt-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">A print-out, not a page</p>
    <h1>The BIP-39 word table</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Every one of the {tableRowCount.toLocaleString()} words in the standard list, reachable from
      {DICE_PER_WORD} throws of an ordinary die and one coin flip. Print it, or open it on a second
      screen. The procedure is on <a href="/roll-your-own-seed">the tool page</a>.
    </p>
    <div class="dwt-how">
      <p><strong>Throw the die {DICE_PER_WORD} times, rerolling any 5 or 6, then flip a coin once.</strong>
      In the BIP-39 word table, find the block headed by your first {PREFIX_THROWS} throws, then read down to your remaining
      {DICE_PER_WORD - PREFIX_THROWS} throws and your flip.</p>
      <p><strong class="mono">H</strong> is heads and <strong class="mono">T</strong> is tails. The die gives
      you {DICE_PER_WORD * 2} of the 11 bits each word needs and the coin gives the last one — which is why
      there are exactly two lines for every dice combination, and why the coin is not optional.</p>
      <p class="dwt-note">The BIP-39 word table is public and contains no secret — it is the standard wordlist in a
      fixed order. Your <em>throws</em> and the <em>words</em> they give you are the secret.</p>
      <p class="dwt-note"><strong>This is the same word table
      <a href="https://bitbox.swiss/bitbox02/BitBox_Diceware_LookupTable.pdf" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">BitBox publishes</a></strong>
      for its own devices. We generated ours independently from the word list and then checked every one of the
      {tableRowCount.toLocaleString()} entries against theirs — all of them match. Two tables built separately
      agreeing on every row is the reason to trust either. Use one of them for a whole seed, though, not a
      mix.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="dwt-print"><button type="button" id="dwt-print" class="btn">Print the table + worksheets</button>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={pdfHref("/dice-word-table.pdf")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open the PDF</a></p>
  </section>

  {/* THE METHOD SHEET — print only, ONE PER SEED LENGTH, its own page each.
      It used to be a single sheet carrying both lengths — "twenty-three words
      for a 24-word seed, or eleven for a 12-word seed" — which makes every
      reader do arithmetic to find their own half, on a sheet they are holding
      while handling key material. Each sheet now states one number, and it is
      theirs. That is also what lets the per-length PDFs be genuinely per-length:
      before this, both documents shared a page that talked about the other one.
      The table pages stay strictly reference; no instructions interleaved with
      2,048 rows. But the print-out cannot be method-less either, because the
      reader is holding paper away from the screen, and one thing here is not
      inferable from the table at all — that 5s and 6s are rerolled, which is why
      no heading contains one.
      It names the site on purpose: this sheet gets passed around, and whoever
      receives it should land on the full procedure rather than work from a
      summary. */}
  {WORKSHEETS.map((ws) => (
    <section class="dwt-sheet">
      <h2 class="ds-h">Rolling a {ws.words}-word seed — the method</h2>
      <p class="ds-lead">
        This sheet explains how to use the BIP-39 word table on the pages after the worksheet, for a
        <strong> {ws.words}-word seed</strong>. The full procedure, including what to do with the words once you
        have them, is at <strong>bitcoinkeys.guide/roll-your-own-seed</strong> — read it before you begin. Do
        not work from this sheet alone.
      </p>

      <h3 class="ds-sh">What you need</h3>
      <p>One six-sided die, one coin, pen and paper. No computer, no phone.</p>

      <h3 class="ds-sh">How many words you roll</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>{ws.chosen} words.</strong> Your seed is {ws.words} words long, and you roll all but the last
        one. <strong>You do not roll word {ws.words}.</strong> It carries a checksum that has to be calculated,
        so there is no entry for it in the table and no way to reach it with a die — your hardware wallet works
        it out, and the procedure on the site explains how for each device.
      </p>

      <h3 class="ds-sh">For each of your {ws.chosen} words</h3>
      <ol class="ds-steps">
        <li><strong>Throw the die {DICE_PER_WORD} times.</strong> <strong>Reroll every 5 and 6</strong> — they
          do not count and are not written down. Keep throwing until you have {DICE_PER_WORD} results that are
          each 1, 2, 3 or 4. This is why no entry in the BIP-39 word table contains a 5 or a 6.</li>
        <li><strong>Flip the coin once.</strong> <span class="mono">H</span> is heads,
          <span class="mono">T</span> is tails. The coin is not optional: the die gives ten of the eleven bits a
          word needs and the coin gives the eleventh. Always taking the same side halves the randomness of every
          word you pick.</li>
        <li><strong>Find your word.</strong> Blocks are headed by your first {PREFIX_THROWS} throws. Find that
          block, then read down to the line matching your remaining
          {DICE_PER_WORD - PREFIX_THROWS} throws and your flip.</li>
        <li><strong>Write the word down, in order, by hand.</strong> Never type it, photograph it, or read it
          aloud in earshot of anything with a microphone.</li>
      </ol>

      <h3 class="ds-sh">Two things worth knowing</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>The BIP-39 word table holds no secret.</strong> It is the standard word list in a fixed order,
        and anyone can regenerate it. Printing it, photographing it or keeping it on a phone is harmless. Your
        <em>throws</em> and the <em>words</em> they produce are the secret, and those never touch a screen.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>This is the same method and the same table BitBox publishes</strong> for its own devices. We
        generated ours independently from the word list and checked all {tableRowCount.toLocaleString()} entries
        against theirs: every one matches. Either table gives the same word for the same throws — but use one
        table for a whole seed, not a mix.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="dwt-work">
      <h2 class="ds-h">Worksheet — {ws.words}-word seed</h2>
      <p class="dw-warn">
        <strong>The moment you write on this sheet it becomes your seed.</strong> Treat it exactly as you would
        the words themselves: never photograph it, never type it up, never leave it out. When your words are
        safely on their permanent backup and you have restored from that backup successfully, destroy this sheet
        — shred or burn it. Do not simply bin it.
      </p>
      {/* THE THROW BOXES ARE GROUPED 3 + 2, matching how the table is actually
          read: the first three throws find the block, the last two plus the flip
          find the line inside it. A flat run of five boxes made the reader hold
          that split in their head while looking at a sheet that did not show it.
          THE GAP IS A REAL COLUMN, not a white shadow painted over the boxes.
          It used to be `box-shadow: -4pt 0 0 0 #fff` on the fourth box, and a
          4pt overlay against 2pt of border-spacing ate 2pt of the THIRD box's
          right edge — every row on the sheet had one box with a missing side.
          A spacer cell cannot paint over its neighbour.
          Row bands every five keep your place — the mistake this artifact
          exists to prevent is a word written one line off. */}
      <table class="dw">
        <thead>
          {/* TWO LINES, and the second one IS the example. Labels carry their own
              parenthetical rather than a separate sub-caption line, and the
              example sits directly beneath them as the header's second row —
              so the sheet explains itself in the space it already had, with no
              extra row for the eye to mistake for an entry. */}
          <tr class="dw-head">
            <th class="dw-n">Word #</th>
            <th class="dw-sp" aria-hidden="true"></th>
            <th colspan={PREFIX_THROWS}>Block <span class="dw-th-s">(1st {PREFIX_THROWS} rolls)</span></th>
            <th class="dw-sp" aria-hidden="true"></th>
            <th colspan={DICE_PER_WORD - PREFIX_THROWS}>Line <span class="dw-th-s">(next {DICE_PER_WORD - PREFIX_THROWS})</span></th>
            <th class="dw-sp" aria-hidden="true"></th>
            <th class="dw-f">Flip <span class="dw-th-s">(H/T)</span></th>
            <th class="dw-sp" aria-hidden="true"></th>
            <th class="dw-w">Word <span class="dw-th-s">(BIP-39 table)</span></th>
          </tr>
          <tr class="dw-eg-row">
            <td class="dw-n"></td>
            <td class="dw-sp"></td>
            {egFaces(EG.dice.slice(0, PREFIX_THROWS)).map((f) => (<td class="dw-die">{f}</td>))}
            <td class="dw-sp"></td>
            {egFaces(EG.dice.slice(PREFIX_THROWS)).map((f) => (<td class="dw-die">{f}</td>))}
            <td class="dw-sp"></td>
            <td class="dw-die dw-coin"><span class="dw-coin-o">{EG.coin}</span></td>
            <td class="dw-sp"></td>
            <td class="dw-ghost">{EG.word}</td>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {Array.from({ length: ws.chosen }, (_, i) => (
            <tr class:list={[(i + 1) % 5 === 0 ? 'dw-five' : null]}>
              <td class="dw-n">{i + 1}</td>
              <td class="dw-sp"></td>
              {Array.from({ length: PREFIX_THROWS }, () => (<td class="dw-b"></td>))}
              <td class="dw-sp"></td>
              {Array.from({ length: DICE_PER_WORD - PREFIX_THROWS }, () => (<td class="dw-b"></td>))}
              <td class="dw-sp"></td>
              <td class="dw-b dw-f"></td>
              <td class="dw-sp"></td>
              <td class="dw-w"></td>
            </tr>
          ))}
          {/* THE SPAN HAS TO STOP AT THE SECTION EDGES. It ran from the spacer
              BEFORE Block to the spacer AFTER Flip, so the grey field overhung
              the columns on both sides and lined up with nothing. It now covers
              Block, Line and Flip exactly — the three sections you would have
              filled in for this word if you rolled it — with the same spacers
              either side that every other row has. */}
          <tr class="dw-last">
            <td class="dw-n">{ws.words}</td>
            <td class="dw-sp"></td>
            <td class="dw-note" colspan={DICE_PER_WORD + 3}>Not rolled — your device calculates this one</td>
            <td class="dw-sp"></td>
            <td class="dw-w"></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="dw-foot">
        <strong>This sheet is for a {ws.words}-word seed:</strong> you roll {ws.chosen} words and your device
        supplies word {ws.words}, which carries the checksum. A {ws.other}-word seed has its own sheet —
        <strong>print only the one you are doing.</strong> Full procedure:
        <strong>bitcoinkeys.guide/roll-your-own-seed</strong>
      </p>
    </section>
  ))}

  {/* THE REFERENCE TABLE GETS A NAME, because the print-out has two kinds of
      page in it and "the table" was doing duty for both. A reader holding six
      sheets could not tell from the artifact which one a sentence meant. The
      reference pages are the BIP-39 WORD TABLE; the ones with boxes are the
      WORKSHEET, and every reference on this site now says which. */}
  <h2 class="dwt-title">BIP-39 word table <span class="dwt-title-s">{tableRowCount.toLocaleString()} words · {DICE_PER_WORD} throws and a coin flip · nothing to fill in</span></h2>
  <section class="dwt-wrap">
    {blocks.map((b) => (
      <div class="dwt-block">
        <h2 class="dwt-h">{b.prefix}<span class="dwt-hx">·</span></h2>
        <ul class="dwt-rows">
          {b.items.map((r) => (
            <li><span class="dwt-k mono">{r.dice}{r.coin}</span>{r.word}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>
    ))}
  </section>
</Base>

<script>
  const b = document.getElementById('dwt-print');
  if (b) b.addEventListener('click', () => window.print());
</script>

<style>
  .dwt-sheet, .dwt-work { display: none; }
  .dwt-how { border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius); padding: 1rem 1.2rem; margin: 1.2rem 0; }
  .dwt-how p { margin: 0 0 .5rem; }
  .dwt-note { color: var(--muted); font-size: .9rem; margin: 0 !important; }
  .dwt-print { display: flex; gap: .7rem; flex-wrap: wrap; margin-top: 1rem; }
  .btn { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; background: var(--accent); color: var(--accent-ink);
    font-weight: 700; font-family: var(--mono); font-size: .9rem; padding: .65rem 1rem;
    border-radius: .6rem; text-decoration: none; border: none; cursor: pointer; }
  .btn.ghost { background: transparent; color: var(--text); border: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .btn.ghost:hover { border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--accent); }

  /* One compact line per word rather than a table. A 3-cell table row carries
     its own padding and borders 2,048 times over, which is what turned this
     into a 26-page print-out. Same markup for screen and print — a separate
     print-only rendering of 2,048 rows is two places for a word to be wrong. */
  .dwt-wrap { columns: 4 13rem; column-gap: 1.5rem; margin-top: 1.4rem; }
  .dwt-block { break-inside: avoid-column; margin-bottom: .8rem; }
  .dwt-h { font-size: .74rem; margin: 0 0 .15rem; letter-spacing: .06em; color: var(--muted);
    font-family: var(--mono); font-weight: 600; }
  .dwt-hx { opacity: .45; margin-left: .3rem; }
  .dwt-rows { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; font-size: .76rem; line-height: 1.35; }
  .dwt-rows li { white-space: nowrap; }
  .dwt-k { color: var(--muted); margin-right: .4rem; }

  @media print {
    /* Ink and paper. Everything above the table is screen furniture; the
       method lives on the procedure page, which is where a reader who needs
       it is standing anyway. */
    /* WHITE PAPER, and no dead padding. The site's cream sat behind the method
       sheet and both worksheets — a full-bleed tint on every sheet, ink spent on
       nothing. <main>'s padding is breathing room under a sticky nav and is
       blank paper once the nav is hidden. */
    :root, body, main { background: #fff !important; }
    main { padding: 0 !important; margin: 0 !important; }
    .verified { display: none !important; }
    .dwt-intro { display: none; }
    /* Its own page, so the reference pages that follow carry nothing but
       the table. */
    .dwt-sheet { display: block; color: #000; break-after: page; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.45; }
    .dwt-work { display: block; color: #000; break-after: page; font-size: 10pt; }
    .dw-warn { border: 1pt solid #000; padding: .4rem .6rem; margin: 0 0 .6rem; font-size: 9pt; line-height: 1.35; }
    .dw { width: 100%; border-collapse: separate; border-spacing: 2pt 2.5pt; font-size: 9pt; }
    /* WHITE, EXPLICITLY. The site's global table styling gives every th the
       surface cream, which survived the page-level white override and left a
       warm band straight across the top of an otherwise white sheet — 5,200
       stray pixels, found by taking a colour census of the rendered page rather
       than by looking at it. The FLIP column keeps its grey on purpose: that
       tint marks the column, and it should run through the header too. */
    /* EVERY HEADING ON EQUAL FOOTING. Flip carried the column's grey and read
       as a different KIND of heading from the others — it is not, it is one of
       five labels. The tint stays on the flip BOXES, where it marks the column
       the reader has to remember is not a die. */
    /* EACH LABEL ON ONE LINE, CENTRED OVER ITS OWN SECTION. The descriptor used
       to be a block element under the label, which made the "first line across"
       two lines deep. It is inline and parenthesised now — Block (1st 3 rolls) —
       and each heading centres over the columns it names, so the label sits above
       the boxes it belongs to rather than above the left edge of them. */
    .dw th { font-size: 7.4pt; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .06em; font-weight: 700;
      padding: 0 .1rem .2rem; text-align: center; color: #000; border: none; background: #fff;
      white-space: nowrap; }
    /* Right, to sit over the numbers below it, which are right-aligned too. */
    .dw th.dw-n { text-align: right; }
    /* The label carries the instruction, so the sheet says how to use itself. */
    .dw-th-s { text-transform: none; letter-spacing: 0; font-weight: 400;
      font-size: 6.6pt; color: #555; }
    /* Boxes, not table cells. A hairline box with real space around it reads as
       somewhere to write; a ruled grid reads as a spreadsheet, and people write
       smaller and straddle the lines. */
    /* 21pt is the CEILING, not a preference, and it MOVED — it was 22pt while
       the 24-word worksheet held exactly 24 rows. The worked-example row makes
       25, and at 22pt the sheet's footer spills onto a page of its own: eight
       pages for a seven-page artifact, with page four holding one paragraph.
       Measured against the rebuilt PDF at 22pt (8 pages) and 21pt (7). Do not
       raise it by eye, and re-measure if a row is ever added or removed —
       adding one row is exactly what invalidated the previous ceiling. */
    /* THE SAME TWO GREYS THE BIP-39 SHEET USES, so the two artifacts read as one
       set rather than two documents that happen to be stapled together. Pale
       enough to write over in pencil or biro without fighting the ink. */
    .dw td { border: .5pt solid #c9c9c9; border-radius: 2.5pt; height: 21pt; padding: 0 .2rem;
      background: #f7f7f7; }
    /* THE ROW NUMBER IS A LABEL, NOT A BOX. No fill, no border, centred against
       the row it names rather than sitting on its baseline, and big enough to
       find with a die in the other hand — it is what you check when you lose
       your place, which is the mistake this sheet exists to prevent. */
    /* AS NARROW AS "WORD #" ALLOWS AND NO NARROWER — nowrap on the heading is
       what holds it, so the column cannot squeeze the label onto two lines. The
       number itself is 12pt: it is the thing you check when you lose your place,
       and losing your place is the mistake this sheet exists to prevent. The gap
       to the first box is a real spacer column, not padding, so it matches the
       gaps between the other sections. */
    .dw-n { width: 5%; border: none !important; background: none !important;
      text-align: right; font-size: 12pt; color: #333; font-weight: 700;
      padding-right: 0; vertical-align: middle; }
    .dw-b { width: 5.4%; }
    /* Wide enough for FLIP (H/T) on one line — the narrow column was what forced
       the whole header row to wrap. */
    .dw-f { width: 8.5%; background: #e6e6e6; }
    /* Narrower than it was. The word column had 38% for words averaging six
       letters, and the space is worth more on the left where the numbers and the
       gaps between sections had none to spare. */
    .dw-w { width: 26%; }
    /* THE 3 + 2 SPLIT, DRAWN AS A REAL COLUMN. An empty borderless cell between
       the third and fourth box: the block lookup and the line lookup are two
       different operations and the sheet should show that.
       IT USED TO BE AN OVERLAY — `box-shadow: -4pt 0 0 0 #fff` on the fourth
       box — and against 2pt of border-spacing a 4pt white shadow reached 2pt
       PAST the gap and painted out the right edge of the third box. Every row
       on every printed sheet had one box with a missing side. A spacer cell
       occupies space instead of covering it, so it cannot do that to a
       neighbour; do not go back to a shadow. */
    /* ONE GAP BETWEEN EVERY SECTION, none within them. There was a wide gap
       between Block and Line and nothing anywhere else, so the sheet showed one
       of its four boundaries and hid the other three. Block | Line | Flip | Word
       are four separate things you fill in at four different moments; the boxes
       inside each are one thing. The spacing should say that, and now it does.
       A real column, never a shadow painted over a neighbour — see the note on
       the box fill above. */
    .dw td.dw-sp, .dw th.dw-sp { width: 2.6%; border: none !important; background: none !important;
      padding: 0; }
    /* The dice faces on the column headers. Only 1-4 are ever shown: 5s and 6s
       are rerolled and appear nowhere in the table. */
    .dw-pips { font-size: 11pt; letter-spacing: .08em; color: #222; text-transform: none;
      font-weight: 400; line-height: 1; margin-left: .12rem; vertical-align: -.08em; }
    /* Mixed faces on purpose. Three identical dice next to an example row full
       of digits reads as "roll these numbers"; a spread of faces reads as
       "three dice", which is the whole job. Never a 5 or a 6 — those are
       rerolled and appear nowhere in the table or on this sheet. */
    /* The worked example. Boxed rather than run in as prose so the eye can find
       it while holding a die, and light enough not to compete with the warning
       directly above it. */
    /* The example row. Grey and italic so it cannot be mistaken for a row to
       write in, and its height matches the rest so the sheet still reads as a
       grid. It is the first thing under the header for a reason: the reader
       sees one completed lookup before filling the first empty box. */
    /* THE EXAMPLE LINE IS NOT A ROW YOU COULD WRITE IN, and that is the whole
       design. It printed digits inside bordered boxes — the same shape as the
       rows below it — so a glance read it as somebody's actual first word
       already sitting on the sheet. No boxes now: just the dice you threw, the
       coin, and the word they give you, ghosted. Nothing labels it as an
       example, because nothing needs to: dice are objects and a grey word is
       obviously not one you wrote. A reader who pauses over it works out what
       it is, and that pause is the explanation. */
    .dw-eg-row td { border: none !important; background: none !important; height: 15pt;
      text-align: center; padding: 0 0 .12rem; vertical-align: middle; }
    .dw-eg-row .dw-die { font-size: 14pt; color: #333; line-height: 1; }
    .dw-eg-row .dw-coin-o { display: inline-block; width: 11pt; height: 11pt; line-height: 10.4pt;
      border: .5pt solid #888; border-radius: 50%; font-size: 7.4pt; color: #444;
      font-family: var(--mono); text-align: center; }
    .dw-eg-row .dw-ghost { text-align: center; font-size: 8.5pt;
      color: #a8a8a8; font-style: italic; }
    /* Place-keeping. Every fifth row is tinted so a glance tells you which
       ten you are in — the failure this sheet exists to prevent is a word
       written one line off. */
    /* Place-keeping every fifth row. It has to sit BELOW the box fill now that
       the boxes are shaded, so it tints the row rather than the box. */
    .dw-five td { background: #ececec; }
    .dw-five .dw-f { background: #dcdcdc; }
    .dw-five .dw-n, .dw-five .dw-sp { background: none !important; }
    /* .dw-mark is GONE with the single-sheet worksheet. It drew a rule across
       row 11 to mark where a 12-word seed stopped, on a sheet that also had to
       serve 24-word readers. There are two sheets now and neither needs a line
       through it — each has exactly its own rows. */
